"Internal Error ()\nciteturn944view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2025/papers/Wang_VGGT_Visual_Geometry_Grounded_Transformer_CVPR_2025_paper.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2025/papers/Wang_VGGT_Visual_Geometry_Grounded_Transformer_CVPR_2025_paper.pdf: (403) Forbidden\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://proceedings.nips.cc/paper/2021/file/89fcd07f20b6785b92134bd6c1d0fa42-Paper.pdf)\nciteturn944view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://proceedings.nips.cc/paper/2021/file/89fcd07f20b6785b92134bd6c1d0fa42-Paper.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 666\nL0@P0: DROID-SLAM: Deep Visual SLAM for Monocular,\nL1@P0: Stereo, and RGB-D Cameras\nL2@P0: Zachary Teed Jia Deng\nL3@P0: Princeton University\nL4@P0: {zteed,jiadeng}@princeton.edu\nL5@P0: Abstract\nL6@P0: We introduce DROID-SLAM, a new deep learning based SLAM system. DROID\u0002SLAM consists of recurrent iterative updates of camera pose and pixelwise depth\nL7@P0: through a Dense Bundle Adjustment layer. DROID-SLAM is accurate, achieving\nL8@P0: large improvements over prior work, and robust, suffering from substantially fewer\nL9@P0: catastrophic failures. Despite training on monocular video, it can leverage stereo\nL10@P0: or RGB-D video to achieve improved performance at test time. The URL to our\nL11@P0: open source code is https://github.com/princeton-vl/DROID-SLAM.\nL12@P0: 1 Introduction\nL13@P0: Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) aims to (1) build a map of the environment and\nL14@P0: (2) localize the agent within the environment. It is a special form of Structure-from-Motion (SfM)\nL15@P0: focused on accurate tracking of long-term trajectories. It is a critical capability for robotics, especially\nL16@P0: autonomous vehicles. In this work, we address visual SLAM, where sensor recordings come in the\nL17@P0: form of images captured from a monocular, stereo, or RGB-D camera.\nL18@P0: The SLAM problem has been approached from a number of different angles. Early work was built\nL19@P0: using probabilistic and filtering based approaches [12, 30], and alternating optimization of the map\nL20@P0: and camera poses [34, 16]. More recently, modern SLAM systems have leveraged least-squares\nL21@P0: optimization. A key element for accuracy has been full Bundle Adjustment (BA), which jointly\nL22@P0: optimizes the camera poses and the 3D map in a single optimization problem. One advantage of the\nL23@P0: optimization-based formulation is that a SLAM system can be easily modified to leverage different\nL24@P0: sensors. For example, ORB-SLAM3 [5] supports monocular, stereo, RGB-D, and IMU sensors, and\nL25@P0: modern systems can support a variety of camera models [5, 27, 42, 6]. Despite significant progress,\nL26@P0: current SLAM systems lack the robustness demanded for many real-world applications. Failures\nL27@P0: come in many forms, such as lost feature tracks, divergence in the optimization algorithm, and\nL28@P0: accumulation of drift.\nL29@P0: Deep learning has been proposed as a solution to many of these failure cases. Previous work\nL30@P0: has investigated replacing hand-crafted with learned features[13, 7, 29, 26, 35], using neural 3D\nL31@P0: representations[46, 1, 9, 45, 44, 25, 22], and combining learned energy terms with classical op\u0002timization backends[58, 57]. Other work has tried to learn SLAM or VO systems end-to-end\nL32@P0: [59, 47, 53, 52, 46]. While these systems are sometimes more robust, they fall far short of the\nL33@P0: accuracy of their classical counterparts on common benchmarks.\nL34@P0: In this work we introduce DROID-SLAM, a new SLAM system based on deep learning. It has\nL35@P0: state-of-the-art performance, outperforming existing SLAM systems, classical or learning-based, on\nL36@P0: challenging benchmarks with very large margins. In particular, it has the following advantages:\nL37@P0: • High Accuracy: We achieve large improvements over prior work across multiple datasets\nL38@P0: and modalities. On the TartanAir SLAM competition [54], we reduce error by 62% over the\nL39@P0: best prior result on the monocular track and 60% on the stereo track. We rank 1st on the\nL40@P0-1: 35th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2021).DROID-SLAM\nL41@P1: Monocular, Stereo or RGB-D Video\nL42@P1: Figure 1: DROID-SLAM can operate on monocular, stereo, and RGB-D video. It builds a dense 3D\nL43@P1: map of the environment while simultaneously localizing the camera within the map.\nL44@P1: ETH-3D RGB-D SLAM leaderboard [41], outperforming the second place by 35% under\nL45@P1: the AUC metric which considers both error and rate of catastrophic failure. On EuRoC [2],\nL46@P1: with monocular input, we reduce error by 82% among methods with zero failures, and by\nL47@P1: 43% over ORB-SLAM3 considering only the 10 out of 11 sequences it succeeds on. With\nL48@P1: stereo input, we reduce error by 71% over ORB-SLAM3. On TUM-RGBD [43], we reduce\nL49@P1: error by 83% among the methods with zero failures.\nL50@P1: • High Robustness: We have substantially fewer catastrophic failures than prior systems. On\nL51@P1: ETH-3D, we successfully track 30 of the 32 RGB-D datasets, while the next best successfully\nL52@P1: tracks only 19/32. On TartanAir, EuRoC, and TUM-RGBD, we have zero failures.\nL53@P1: • Strong Generalization: Our system, trained only with monocular input, can directly use\nL54@P1: stereo or RGB-D input to get improved accuracy without any retraining. All of our results\nL55@P1: across 4 datasets and 3 modalities are achieved by a single model, trained once with only\nL56@P1: monocular input entirely on the synthetic TartanAir dataset.\nL57@P1: The strong performance and generalization of DROID-SLAM is made possible by its “Differentiable\nL58@P1: Recurrent Optimization-Inspired Design” (DROID), which is an end-to-end differentiable architecture\nL59@P1: that combines the strengths of both classical approaches and deep networks. Specifically, it consists\nL60@P1: of recurrent iterative updates, building upon RAFT [48] for optical flow but introducing two key\nL61@P1: innovations.\nL62@P1: First, unlike RAFT, which iteratively updates optical flow, we iteratively update camera poses and\nL63@P1: depth. Whereas RAFT operates on two frames, our updates are applied to an arbitrary number of\nL64@P1: frames, enabling joint global refinement of all camera poses and depth maps, essential for minimizing\nL65@P1: drift for long trajectories and loop closures.\nL66@P1: Second, each update of camera poses and depth maps in DROID-SLAM is produced by a differentiable\nL67@P1: Dense Bundle Adjustment (DBA) layer, which computes a Gauss-Newton update to camera poses\nL68@P1: and dense per-pixel depth so as to maximize their compatibility with the current estimate of optical\nL69@P1: flow. This DBA layer leverages geometric constraints, improves accuracy and robustness, and enables\nL70@P1: a monocular system to handle stereo or RGB-D input without retraining.\nL71@P1: The design of DROID-SLAM is novel. The closest prior deep architectures are DeepV2D [47]\nL72@P1: and BA-Net [46], both of which were focused on depth estimation and reported limited SLAM\nL73@P1: results. DeepV2D alternates between updating depth and updating camera poses, instead of bundle\nL74@P1: adjustment. BA-Net has a bundle adjustment layer, but their layer is substantially different: it is not\nL75@P1: “dense” in that it optimizes over a small number of coefficients used to linearly combine a depth\nL76@P1: basis (a set of pre-predicted depth maps), whereas we optimize over per-pixel depth directly, without\nL77@P1: being handicapped by a depth basis. In addition, BA-Net optimizes photometric reprojection error (in\nL78@P1: feature space), whereas we optimize geometric error, leveraging state-of-the-art flow estimation.\nL79@P1: We perform extensive evaluation across four different datasets and three different sensor modalities,\nL80@P1: demonstrating state-of-the-art performance in all cases. We also include ablation studies that shed\nL81@P1: light on important design decisions and hyperparameters.\nL82@P1: 2 Related Work\nL83@P1: Modern SLAM systems treat localization and mapping as a joint optimization problem [4].\nL84@P1-2: 2Visual SLAM focuses on observations in the form of monocular, stereo, or RGB-D images. These\nL85@P2: approaches are commonly categorized as either being direct or indirect [15]. Indirect approaches [31,\nL86@P2: 32, 5, 37] first process the image into an intermediate representation by detecting points of interest\nL87@P2: and attaching feature descriptors. Features are then matched between images. Indirect approaches\nL88@P2: optimize camera pose and a 3D point cloud by minimizing reprojection error–the distance between a\nL89@P2: projected 3D point and its location in the image.\nL90@P2: Direct approaches model the image formation process and define an objective function over photomet\u0002ric error [16, 15, 60]. One advantage of direct approaches is that they can model more information\nL91@P2: about the image, such as lines and intensity variations[15] which are not used by indirect approaches.\nL92@P2: However, photometric errors typically lead to more difficult optimization problems, and direct ap\u0002proaches are less robust to geometric distortion such as rolling shutter artifacts. This approach\nL93@P2: requires more sophisticated optimization techniques, such as coarse-to-fine image pyramids to avoid\nL94@P2: local minimum.\nL95@P2: Our method does not clearly fit into either of the categories. Like the direct approach, we do not\nL96@P2: require preprocessing steps to detect and match features between the images. We instead use the full\nL97@P2: image, allowing us to leverage a wider range of information than indirect methods with typically only\nL98@P2: use corners and edges. However, we minimize reprojection error similar to indirect methods. This\nL99@P2: is an easier optimization problem and avoids the need for more complicated representations such\nL100@P2: as image pyramids. In this sense, our approach borrows the best of both approaches: the smoother\nL101@P2: objective function of indirect approaches with the greater modeling capacity of indirect approaches.\nL102@P2: Deep Learning has more recently been applied to the SLAM problem. Many works have focused\nL103@P2: on training systems for particular subproblems, such as feature detection [13, 7, 29, 26, 35], feature\nL104@P2: matching and outlier rejection [38, 36], and localization [51, 39]. SuperGlue [38] was designed to\nL105@P2: perform feature matching and verification and make 2-view pose estimate much more robust. Our\nL106@P2: network also draws inspiration from Dusmanu et al[14], which builds a neural network into the SfM\nL107@P2: pipeline to improve keypoint localization accuracy.\nL108@P2: Other works have focused on training SLAM systems end-to-end [59, 46, 8, 50, 24, 47, 53]. These\nL109@P2: methods are not full SLAM systems, but instead focus on small scale reconstruction on the order\nL110@P2: of two [8, 50, 53] up to a dozen frames [59, 46, 47]. They lack many of the core capabilities\nL111@P2: of modern SLAM systems such as loop closure and global bundle adjustment which inhibit their\nL112@P2: ability to perform large scale reconstruction as demonstrated in our experiments. ∇SLAM[23]\nL113@P2: implements several existing SLAM algorithms as differentiable computation graphs, allowing for\nL114@P2: errors in the reconstruction to be backpropagated back to sensor measurements. While this approach\nL115@P2: is differentiable, it has no trainable parameters, meaning the performance of the system is limited by\nL116@P2: the accuracy of the classical algorithm they emulate.\nL117@P2: DeepFactors[9] is the most complete deep SLAM system, building on the earlier CodeSLAM[1]. It\nL118@P2: performs joint optimization of the pose and depth variables, and is capable of short and long-range\nL119@P2: loop closure. Similar to BA-Net[46], DeepFactors optimizes the parameters of a learned depth basis\nL120@P2: during inference. In contrast, we do not rely on a learned basis, but instead optimize pixelwise depth.\nL121@P2: This allows our network to better generalize to new datasets since our depth representation is not tied\nL122@P2: to the training dataset.\nL123@P2: 3 Approach\nL124@P2: We take a video as input with two objectives: estimate the trajectory of the camera and build a 3D\nL125@P2: map of the environment. We first describe the monocular setting; in Sec. 3.4 we describe how to\nL126@P2: generalize the system to stereo and RGB-D video.\nL127@P2: Representation: Our network operates on an ordered collection of images, {It}\nL128@P2: N\nL129@P2: t=0. For each image\nL130@P2: t, we maintain two state variables: camera pose Gt ∈ SE(3) and inverse depth dt ∈ R\nL131@P2: H×W\nL132@P2: + . The\nL133@P2: set of poses, {Gt}\nL134@P2: N\nL135@P2: t=0, and set of inverse depths {dt}\nL136@P2: N\nL137@P2: t=0 are unknown state variables, which get\nL138@P2: iteratively updated during inference as new frames are processed. For the reminder of the paper, when\nL139@P2: we refer to depths, note that we are using the inverse depth parameterization.\nL140@P2: We adopt a frame-graph (V, E) to represent co-visibility between frames. An edge (i, j) ∈ E means\nL141@P2: image Ii and Ij have overlapping fields of view which shared points. The frame graph is built\nL142@P2: dynamically during training and inference. After each pose or depth update, we can recompute\nL143@P2-3: 3ConvGRU\nL144@P3: DBA\nL145@P3: ConvGRU\nL146@P3: DBA\nL147@P3: Figure 2: Illustration of the update operator. The operator acts on edges in the frame graph, predicting\nL148@P3: flow revisions which are mapped to depth and pose update through the (DBA) layer.\nL149@P3: visibility to update the frame graph. If the camera returns to a previously mapped region, we add\nL150@P3: long range connections in the graph to perform loop closure.\nL151@P3: 3.1 Feature Extraction and Correlation\nL152@P3: Features are extracted from each new image added to they system. Key components of this stage are\nL153@P3: borrowed from RAFT[48].\nL154@P3: Feature Extraction Each of the input images are processed by a feature extraction network. The\nL155@P3: network consists of 6 residual blocks and 3 downsampling layers, producing dense feature maps at\nL156@P3: 1/8 the input image resolution. Like RAFT[48], we use two separate networks: a feature network\nL157@P3: and a context network. The feature network is used to build the set of correlation volumes, while the\nL158@P3: context features are injected into the network during each application of the update operator.\nL159@P3: Correlation Pyramid For each edge in the frame graph, (i, j) ∈ E, we compute a 4D correlation\nL160@P3: volume by taking the dot product between all-pairs of feature vectors in gθ(Ii) and gθ(Ij )\nL161@P3: C\nL162@P3: ij\nL163@P3: u1v1u2v2 = hgθ(Ii)u1v1\nL164@P3: , gθ(Ij )u2v2i (1)\nL165@P3: We then perform average pooling of the last two dimension of the correlation volume following\nL166@P3: RAFT[48] to form a 4-level correlation pyramid.\nL167@P3: Correlation Lookup We define a lookup operator which indexes the correlation volume using a\nL168@P3: grid with radius r, Lr : R\nL169@P3: H×W×H×W × RH×W×2\nL170@P3: 7→ R\nL171@P3: H×W×(r+1)2\nL172@P3: .\nL173@P3: The lookup operator takes an H × W grid of coordinates as input and values are retrieved from the\nL174@P3: correlation volume using bilinear interpolation. The operator is applied to each correlation volume in\nL175@P3: the pyramid and the final feature vector is computed by concatenating the results at each level.\nL176@P3: 3.2 Update Operator\nL177@P3: The core component of our SLAM system is a learned update operator show in Fig. 2. The update\nL178@P3: operator is a 3 × 3 convolutional GRU with hidden state h. Each application of the operator updates\nL179@P3: the hidden state, and additionally produces a pose update, ∆ξ\nL180@P3: (k)\nL181@P3: , and depth update, ∆d\nL182@P3: (k)\nL183@P3: . The pose\nL184@P3: and depth updates are applied to the current depth and pose estimates through retraction on the SE3\nL185@P3: manifold and vector addition respectively\nL186@P3: G(k+1) = Exp(∆ξ\nL187@P3: (k)\nL188@P3: ) ◦ G(k), d\nL189@P3: (k+1) = ∆d(k) + d(k)\nL190@P3: . (2)\nL191@P3: Iterative applications of the update operator produce a sequence of poses and depths, with the expec\u0002tation of converging to a fixed point {G(k)} → G∗\nL192@P3: , {d\nL193@P3: (k)} → d∗\nL194@P3: , reflecting the true reconstruction.\nL195@P3: Correspondence At the start of each iteration we use the current estimates of poses and depths to\nL196@P3: estimate correspondence. Given a grid of pixel coordinates, pi ∈ R\nL197@P3: H×W×2\nL198@P3: in frame i, we compute\nL199@P3: the dense correspondence field pij\nL200@P3: pij = Πc(Gij ◦ Π\nL201@P3: −1\nL202@P3: c\nL203@P3: (pi, di)), pij ∈ R\nL204@P3: H×W×2 Gij = Gj ◦ G−1\nL205@P3: i\nL206@P3: . (3)\nL207@P3-4: 4for each edge (i, j) ∈ E in the frame graph. Here Πc is the camera model mapping a set of 3D\nL208@P4: points onto the image and Π−1\nL209@P4: c\nL210@P4: is the inverse projection function mapping inverse depth map d and\nL211@P4: coordinate grid pito a 3D point cloud (we provide formulas and Jacobians in the appendix). pij\nL212@P4: represents the coordinates of pixels pi mapped into frame j using the estimated pose and depth.\nL213@P4: Inputs We use the correspondence field to index the correlation volumes. For each edge (i, j) ∈ E\nL214@P4: we use pij to perform lookup from the correlation volume Cij to retrieve correlation features.\nL215@P4: Additionally, we use the correspondence field to derive optical flow induced by camera motion as the\nL216@P4: difference pij − pj . Furthermore, the residual from the previous BA solution is concatenated with\nL217@P4: the flow field allowing the network to use feedback from the previous iteration.\nL218@P4: The correlation features provide information about visual similarity in the neighbourhood of pij\nL219@P4: allowing the network to learn to align visually similar image regions. However, correspondence is\nL220@P4: sometimes ambiguous. The flow provides an complementary source of information allowing the\nL221@P4: network to exploit smoothness in the motion fields to gain robustness.\nL222@P4: Update The correlation features and flow features are each mapped through two convolutional layers\nL223@P4: before being injected into the GRU. Additionally, we inject context features, as extracted by the\nL224@P4: context network, into the GRU through element-wise addition.\nL225@P4: The ConvGRU is a local operation with a small receptive field. We extract global context by averaging\nL226@P4: the hidden state across the spatial dimensions of the image and use this feature vector as additional\nL227@P4: input to the GRU. Global context is important in SLAM because incorrect correspondences, caused\nL228@P4: by large moving objects for example, can degrade the accuracy of the system. It is important for the\nL229@P4: network to recognize and reject erroneous correspondence.\nL230@P4: The GRU produces an updated hidden state h\nL231@P4: (k+1). Instead of predicting updates to the depth or\nL232@P4: pose directly, we instead predict updates in the space of dense flow fields. We map the hidden\nL233@P4: state through two additional convoluation layers to produce two outputs: (1) a revision flow field\nL234@P4: rij ∈ R\nL235@P4: H×W×2\nL236@P4: and (2) associated confidence map wij ∈ R\nL237@P4: H×W×2\nL238@P4: + . The revision rij is a correction\nL239@P4: term predicted by the network to correct errors in the dense correspondence field. We denote the\nL240@P4: corrected correspondence as p\nL241@P4: ∗\nL242@P4: ij = rij + pij\nL243@P4: We then pool the hidden state over all features which share the same source view i and predict a\nL244@P4: pixel-wise damping factor λ. We use the softplus operator to ensure that the damping term is positive.\nL245@P4: Additionally, we use the pooled features to predict a 8x8 mask which can be used to upsample the\nL246@P4: inverse depth estimate.\nL247@P4: Dense Bundle Adjustment Layer (DBA) The Dense Bundle Adjustment Layer (DBA) maps the set\nL248@P4: of flow revisions into a set of pose and pixelwise depth updates. We define the cost function over the\nL249@P4: entire frame graph\nL250@P4: E(G0, d\nL251@P4: 0\nL252@P4: ) = X\nL253@P4: (i,j)∈E\nL254@P4: \nL255@P4: \nL256@P4: \nL257@P4: p\nL258@P4: ∗\nL259@P4: ij − Πc(G0ij ◦ Π\nL260@P4: −1\nL261@P4: c\nL262@P4: (pi, d\nL263@P4: 0\nL264@P4: i\nL265@P4: ))\nL266@P4: \nL267@P4: \nL268@P4: \nL269@P4: \nL270@P4: 2\nL271@P4: Σij\nL272@P4: Σij = diag wij . (4)\nL273@P4: where k·kΣis the Mahalanobis distance which weights the error terms based on the confidence\nL274@P4: weights wij . Eqn. 4 states that we want an updated pose G0and depth d\nL275@P4: 0\nL276@P4: such that reprojected points\nL277@P4: match the revised correspondence p\nL278@P4: ∗\nL279@P4: ij as predicted by the update operator.\nL280@P4: We use local parameterization to linearize Eqn. 4 and use the Gauss-Newton algorithm solve for\nL281@P4: updates (∆ξ, ∆d). Since each term in Eqn. 4 only includes a single depth variable, the Hessian\nL282@P4: matrix has block diagonal structure. Separating pose and depth variables, the system can be solved\nL283@P4: efficiently using the Schur complement with the pixelwise damping factor λ added to the depth block\nL284@P4: \u0014\nL285@P4: B E\nL286@P4: ET C\nL287@P4: \u0015 \u0014∆ξ\nL288@P4: ∆d\nL289@P4: \u0015\nL290@P4: =\nL291@P4: \u0014\nL292@P4: v\nL293@P4: w\nL294@P4: \u0015\nL295@P4: ∆ξ = [B − EC−1ET]\nL296@P4: −1\nL297@P4: (v − EC−1w)\nL298@P4: ∆d = C−1(w − ET ∆ξ)\nL299@P4: (5)\nL300@P4: where C is diagonal and can be cheaply inverted C−1 = 1/C. The DBA layer is implemented as\nL301@P4: part of the computation graph and backpropogation is performed through the layer during training.\nL302@P4: 3.3 Training\nL303@P4: Our SLAM system is implemented in PyTorch and we use the LieTorch extension [49] to perform\nL304@P4: backprogation in the tangent space of all group elements.\nL305@P4-5: 5Removing gauge freedom In the monocular setting, the network is only able to recover the trajectory\nL306@P5: of the camera up to a similarity transform. One solution is to define a loss which is invariant to\nL307@P5: similarity transforms. However, the gauge-freedom still exists during training which poorly impacts\nL308@P5: the conditioning of the linear system and the stability of the gradients. We solve this problem by\nL309@P5: fixing the first two poses to the ground-truth poses of each training sequence. Fixing the first pose\nL310@P5: removes the 6-dof gauge freedom. Fixing the second pose resolves the scale freedom.\nL311@P5: Constructing training video Each training example consists of a 7-frame video sequence. In order\nL312@P5: to ensure stable training and good downstream performance, we want to sample videos which are not\nL313@P5: too easy nor too difficult.\nL314@P5: The training set is composed of a collection of videos. For each video i of length Ni, we precompute\nL315@P5: an Ni × Ni distance matrix storing the average optical flow magnitude between each pair of frames.\nL316@P5: However, not all frames are covisible; and frames pairs with less than 50% overlap are assigned\nL317@P5: a distance of infinity. During training, we dynamically generate videos by sampling paths in the\nL318@P5: distance matrix, such that the average flow between adjacent video frames is between 8px and 96px.\nL319@P5: Supervision We supervise our network using a combination of pose loss and flow loss. The flow\nL320@P5: loss is applied to pairs of adjacent frames. We compute the optical flow induced by the predicted\nL321@P5: depth and poses and the flow induced by the ground truth depth and poses. The loss is taken to be the\nL322@P5: average l2 distance between the two flow fields.\nL323@P5: Given a set of ground truth poses {T}\nL324@P5: N\nL325@P5: i\nL326@P5: and predicted poses {G}\nL327@P5: N\nL328@P5: i\nL329@P5: , the pose loss is taken to be the\nL330@P5: distance between the ground truth and predicted poses, Lpose =\nL331@P5: P\nL332@P5: i\nL333@P5: || LogSE3(T\nL334@P5: −1\nL335@P5: i\nL336@P5: · Gi)||2. We\nL337@P5: apply the losses to the output of every iteration with exponentially increasing weight using γ = 0.9.\nL338@P5: 3.4 SLAM System\nL339@P5: During inference, we compose the network into a full SLAM system. The SLAM system takes a video\nL340@P5: stream as input, and performs reconstruction and localization in real-time. Our system contains two\nL341@P5: threads which run asynchronously. The frontend thread takes in new frames, extracts features, selects\nL342@P5: keyframes, and performs local bundle adjustment. The backend thread simultaneously performs\nL343@P5: global bundle adjustment over the entire history of keyframes. We provide an overview of the system\nL344@P5: here, and provide more information in the appendix.\nL345@P5: Initialization Initialization is simple with DROID-SLAM. We simply collect frames until we have\nL346@P5: a set of 12. As we accumulate frames, we only keep the previous frame if optical flow is greater\nL347@P5: than 16px (estimated by applying one update iteration). Once 12 frames have been accumulated, we\nL348@P5: initialize a frame graph by creating an edges between keyframes which are within 3 timesteps apart,\nL349@P5: then run 10 iterations of the update operator.\nL350@P5: Frontend The frontend operates directly on the incoming video stream. It maintains a collection of\nL351@P5: keyframes and a frame graph storing edges between covisible kefyrames. Keyframe poses and depths\nL352@P5: are actively being optimized. Features are first extracted from the incoming frames. The new frame is\nL353@P5: then added to the frame graph adding edges with its 3 closest neighbors as measured by mean optical\nL354@P5: flow. The pose is initialized using a linear motion model. We then apply several iterations of the\nL355@P5: update operator to update keyframe poses and depths. We fix the first two poses to remove gauge\nL356@P5: freedom but treat all depths as free variables.\nL357@P5: After the new frame is tracked, we select a keyframe for removal. We compute distance between\nL358@P5: pairs of frames by computing the average optical flow magnitude and remove redundant frames. If no\nL359@P5: frame is a good candidate for removal, we remove the oldest keyframe.\nL360@P5: Backend The backend performs global bundle adjustment over the entire history of keyframes.\nL361@P5: During each iteration, we rebuild the frame graph using the flow between all pairs of keyframes,\nL362@P5: represented as an N × N distance matrix. We first add edges between temporally adjacent keyframes.\nL363@P5: We then sample new edges from the distance matrix in order of increasing flow. With each selected\nL364@P5: edge, we suppress neighboring edges within a distance of 2, where distance is defined as the\nL365@P5: Chebyshev distance between index pairs ||(i, j) − (k, l)||∞ = max(|i − k|, |j − l|).\nL366@P5: We then apply the update operator to the entire frame graph, often consisting of thousands of frames\nL367@P5: and edges. Storing the full set of correlation volumes would quickly exceed video memory. Instead,\nL368@P5: we use the memory efficient implementation proposed in RAFT [48].\nL369@P5-6: 6Figure 3: DROID-SLAM can generalize to new datasets. In order, we show results from Tanks &\nL370@P6: Temples [21], ScanNet [10], Sintel [3], and ETH-3D [41]; all using monocular video.\nL371@P6: During training, we implement dense bundle adjustment in PyTorch to leverage the automatic\nL372@P6: differentiation engine. At inference time, we use a custom CUDA kernel which takes advantage\nL373@P6: of the block-sparse structure of the problem, then perform sparse Cholesky decomposition on the\nL374@P6: reduced camera block.\nL375@P6: We only perform full bundle adjustment on keyframe images. In order to recover the poses of\nL376@P6: non-keyframes, we perform motion-only bundle adjustment by iteratively estimating flow between\nL377@P6: each keyframe and its neighboring non-keyframes. During testing, we evaluate on the full camera\nL378@P6: trajectory, not just keyframes.\n"

